<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Malacca</h1><p class="st-meta">1641 CE – 1825 CE · capital Malacca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dutch-ruled Malacca, taken from Portugal in 1641, which guarded the Strait of Malacca for the Dutch East India Company for nearly two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> for five months and captured it in January 1641. The Company wanted to secure the strait and divert trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> rather than revive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> as a great emporium, so the port was ruled as a fortified outpost.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor and council ran the city from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stadthuys">Stadthuys</a>, completed around 1660. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> checked shipping in the strait, bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak</a> and other Malay states, and sold Indian cloth. Its population mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> officials, Portuguese Eurasians, Chinese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and Indians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, governed it from 1662 to 1665.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1795 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> and returned it in 1818. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> exchanged it for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bencoolen">Bencoolen</a>, and after the handover in 1825 it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> buildings still dominate its historic centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-malacca.jpg" alt="Dutch Malacca" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1641 CE – 1825 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Malacca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan van Riebeeck, Balthasar Bort</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malacca (Dutch)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=dutch-malacca" aria-label="Dutch Malacca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7519 2096 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Malacca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7839 2352L7843 2352M7839 2352L7959 2663M7839 2352L4997 651M7839 2352L7883 2385"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7843 2352h0M7959 2663h0M4997 651h0M7883 2385h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7839 2352h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7839 2352h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Malacca. <a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=dutch-malacca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
